WEBVTT

00:02.600 --> 00:05.159
This is how BinPar's clinical chat answers:

00:05.785 --> 00:08.801
what happens from the moment the clinician
types the question until they open

00:08.841 --> 00:10.252
the source that supports it.

00:11.680 --> 00:15.776
Each organisation signs in through its
own domain, with its own design.

00:16.069 --> 00:20.443
The clinician's profile, for example
family medicine in primary care,

00:20.865 --> 00:24.850
suggests which specialties to ask,
without hiding any content.

00:26.218 --> 00:27.900
The clinician chooses the mode.

00:27.940 --> 00:32.104
Standard answers sooner; Precision
spends more time on each answer.

00:32.144 --> 00:36.352
Then they type the question,
as they would ask a colleague.

00:37.757 --> 00:40.020
On sending, the context is gathered:

00:40.350 --> 00:44.865
the organisation's committee, the profile,
the earlier conversation and the clinician's

00:44.905 --> 00:46.391
answer preferences.

00:47.016 --> 00:51.069
An evidence map runs through every
corpus and flags which specialties

00:51.109 --> 00:53.002
cover the topic, and in which sections.

00:54.319 --> 00:58.612
With that map, the committee coordination
selects which specialties to ask,

00:58.652 --> 01:00.908
and with which sub-query.

01:00.948 --> 01:04.186
It can consult several at once, and return
to one if its answer leaves something

01:04.226 --> 01:05.426
open.

01:06.152 --> 01:10.668
Each specialty works from its entire corpus,
not loose excerpts from a search,

01:10.708 --> 01:13.693
in the version that applies
to the clinician's centre.

01:14.837 --> 01:16.193
It answers by citing its units.

01:16.578 --> 01:20.248
If the topic is not in its corpus, it says so,
and that answer counts too.

01:21.787 --> 01:25.087
Meanwhile, the clinician
sees the work in progress:

01:25.472 --> 01:29.895
a short line on what is being
consulted, and a card per specialty,

01:29.935 --> 01:32.346
with its status and the
pages it has reviewed.

01:33.065 --> 01:36.031
When the answer starts, it all
collapses into a single line.

01:37.618 --> 01:42.023
If the guideline distinguishes cases and
a value is missing, such as kidney function,

01:42.063 --> 01:44.678
the chat asks before answering,
with closed options.

01:45.304 --> 01:47.567
It never asks about drugs or doses.

01:47.896 --> 01:51.004
The clinician's reply resumes the
query, without repeating the work

01:51.044 --> 01:52.244
already done.

01:53.329 --> 01:57.185
The answer is drafted from the
specialties' contributions.

01:57.792 --> 02:00.956
On closing it, each statement is linked
to the exact unit that supports it,

02:00.996 --> 02:03.407
and anything without a citation is removed.

02:03.996 --> 02:06.462
If the guideline does not cover it,
the answer says so.

02:07.070 --> 02:10.659
General knowledge is added only when
the organisation allows it, and always

02:10.699 --> 02:11.899
flagged.

02:12.705 --> 02:16.135
Any citation opens in its source,
with the passage highlighted.

02:16.335 --> 02:20.239
If the source is a PDF, the viewer shows
the original page and indicates whether

02:20.279 --> 02:23.320
the match is verbatim and whether
the text has been verified.

02:23.835 --> 02:26.913
From there you go back to the answer,
or open the full document.

02:28.527 --> 02:32.438
The conversation continues with the
context of the previous answers.

02:32.749 --> 02:37.012
Every answer can be rated, and if it was
not useful, a comment is required:

02:37.138 --> 02:39.567
that way the team knows what
to correct in the corpus.
